course: history
lesson: 78
title: Humanity today: how many we are, how long we live, how poor and how rich
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: If the whole world were a class of 100 children, about 18 would be from India, about 17 from China, about 10 would be very poor, about 8 would not get enough food, and 3 or 4 would live in a country they were not born in; numbers like these turn «the world» into people you could meet.
explain: The UN says we reached 8 billion people in November 2022 and expects the number to peak at about 10.3 billion in the 2080s and then slowly fall, because families almost everywhere are getting smaller.
explain: People live much longer than in 1900, mostly because far fewer babies and small children die, but where you are born still matters a lot: a child in Japan can expect to live about 30 years longer than a child in Chad.
explain: Extreme poverty has fallen from most of humanity around 1820 to about 1 person in 10 today, and both halves of that sentence matter: huge progress, and hundreds of millions still very poor.
explain: Big numbers are always estimates, and in a war, as in Ukraine since 2022, even counting people becomes hard.
fact: The UN says the world reached 8 billion people on 15 November 2022 and expects about 10.3 billion at the peak in the 2080s, after which the number should slowly fall.
fact: India passed China as the country with the most people in 2023, with more than 1.4 billion.
fact: Average life expectancy in the world was about 32 years in 1900 and is about 73 today, mostly because far fewer children die young; many adults in 1900 lived long lives.
fact: Around 1820 most people lived in extreme poverty; today about 1 person in 10, roughly 700 to 800 million depending on how it is measured, still lives on very little money.
fact: About 700 million people in the world still do not get enough food.
fact: In 1950 women had about five children on average; now the world average is about 2.2, because fewer children die and more girls go to school.
fact: About 3 in 10 people in Japan are over 65, while in Niger about half the people are younger than 15; where there are many old people, fewer young workers have to support more people who have stopped working.
fact: Around 2.2 to 2.6 billion people are still offline, depending on the year and how it is counted.
fact: People in Japan live into their mid-80s on average, while in Chad and some other poor countries life expectancy is in the 50s.
fact: About 300 million people, roughly 1 in 30, live outside the country where they were born; people have always moved, and migration is also argued about in politics.
fact: Children today are healthier and live longer than in the past, but face new worries like climate change and life online.
fact: Since the full-scale invasion in 2022, war, refugees and fewer births have made Ukraine's population hard to count; estimates differ by millions, and about 6 million Ukrainian refugees abroad were reported.
exercise: When did the world reach 8 billion people, according to the UN? | In November 2022 | In 1900 | In 1066 | In 2050
exercise: What does the UN expect world population to do later this century? | Peak at about 10.3 billion and then slowly fall | Grow for ever without stopping, to 100 billion and more | Halve by 2030 | Stay at exactly 8 billion
exercise: Which country has had the most people since 2023? | India | China | The USA | Indonesia
exercise: What was average life expectancy in the world in 1900? | About 32 years | About 82 years | About 12 years | About 102 years
exercise: Why was average life expectancy so low in 1900? | Many children died young, pulling the average down | Every adult died at 32 | People were born old | Nobody counted anyone's age then, so the number is made up
exercise: Around 1820, how many people lived in extreme poverty? | Most people | Almost nobody | Exactly half | Only kings
exercise: About what share of people live in extreme poverty today? | About 1 in 10 | About 9 in 10 | Nobody at all | About 1 in a million
exercise: About how many people still do not get enough food? | About 700 million | About 7,000 | Nobody | About 7 billion
exercise: Why do women have fewer children on average than in 1950? | Fewer children die and more girls go to school | Children were banned by law in most countries of the world | Families just got bored of having lots of children | Hospitals stopped helping mothers have their babies
exercise: What is the world average number of children per woman today? | About 2.2 | About 8 | About 0.5 | About 5
exercise: Which country has about 3 in 10 people over 65? | Japan | Niger | Chad | India
exercise: About how many people are still offline? | Over 2 billion | Nobody | About 200 | Everyone outside the USA
exercise: Why do people in Japan live longer than people in Chad? | Health care, wealth and peace make a big difference | People in Japan are simply born with much stronger hearts | Chad has no hospitals at all | It is only a matter of luck
exercise: About how many people live outside the country where they were born? | About 300 million, roughly 1 in 30 | Nobody, because moving country is forbidden | About half of all the people in the world | About 3,000, mostly famous footballers
exercise: Why is Ukraine's population hard to count since 2022? | War, refugees and fewer births | Ukrainians refuse to be counted | It was counted perfectly last week | Nobody ever counted it before
test: Ivy reads "8 BILLION PEOPLE!!" and her bro goes, "So it'll be 80 billion soon, the planet's cooked." ("Cooked" means doomed.) What's the UN view? | It expects a peak of about 10.3 billion in the 2080s, then a slow fall | It will reach 80 billion by 2040 | It will drop to 1 billion next year | Nobody knows anything about population
test: Raj: "India's got more people than China now, yaar." ("Yaar" means mate, in Indian English.) Is he right? | Yes, India passed China in 2023 | No, China still has twice as many, yaar | No, the USA has the most people of all | Yes, India has had the most since 1500
test: Gran: "In 1900, folk died at 32, pet." What's more exact? | The average was about 32 because many children died; many adults lived long | Everyone died on their 32nd birthday | Nobody lived past 20 | People lived longer then than now
test: Lily: "Extreme poverty dropped? So nobody's poor now, slay!" ("Slay" means great.) What's the honest answer? | Far fewer are very poor than in 1820, but about 1 in 10 still are | Yes, poverty has completely ended | No, poverty has got much worse everywhere in the world since 1820 | Poverty was never measured
test: Ethan: "Is anyone still hungry? Like, actually?" Best answer? | Yes, about 700 million people still do not get enough food | No, hunger ended everywhere in the world a long time ago, in 1900 | Only people who are on special diets are ever hungry these days | Everyone in the world is hungry, all the time, every single day
test: Mum: "My gran had eight kids, I've got two." Grace: "Why'd you stop at two, Mum, mid?" What's the bigger pattern? | Families shrank worldwide as fewer children died and more girls went to school | It was just her own choice and nothing else | Families have grown bigger since 1950 | Large families were banned everywhere
test: Hiroshi: "So many old people in Japan, it's mad!" His Nigerien penpal: "Here everyone's young!" What would happen to a country with many old and few young people? | Fewer workers would support more retired people | Nothing would ever change, the country would stay the same | It would need more and more schools for all the children | Everyone in that country would slowly get younger
test: Amir: "Everyone's got internet, bruv, even my nan's on TikTok." What's true? | Over 2 billion people are still offline | Every person on Earth is online these days | Only grandmas really use the internet now | The internet was switched off in most places in 2020
test: Zara's cousin: "People in Chad die younger 'cause they're just less healthy people, innit." What's fairer? | Health care, wealth and peace make the big difference, not the people themselves | People there are born weaker | It is because of the weather only | People in Chad live longer than in Japan
test: Tom's classmate came from Syria. A boy says, "Migration's a new thing, it never used to happen." What's true? | People have always moved; about 1 in 30 live outside their birth country now | Nobody moved before 2000 | Half the world lives abroad | Moving country is impossible
test: Grandad: "Kids today have it easy!" Ben: "Not with homework, Grandad, that's sus!" Who's right? | Both partly: kids are healthier now, but face new worries | Only Grandad: life is perfect now and children have no worries at all | Only Ben, life was easier in 1900 | Neither, nothing has changed
test: Kai: "How many people are there in Ukraine?" His dad: "Hard to say since the war." Why can't anyone just look it up? | War, refugees and fewer births mean estimates differ by millions | Ukraine has never had a census of any kind, not even in Soviet times | The number is a secret | Everyone has already counted it exactly
exam: Identify the date upon which, according to the United Nations, the population of the world attained eight billion persons. | The fifteenth of November, two thousand and twenty-two | The first of January, nineteen hundred | The fifteenth of November, in the year nineteen hundred and fifty | The first of January, two thousand and fifty
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the low average life expectancy recorded in the year nineteen hundred? | It resulted principally from the high mortality of young children | It resulted from all adults perishing at precisely thirty-two years of age | It was a clerical error of the census | It was higher than at present
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning extreme poverty. | It has declined from the majority of humanity circa eighteen hundred and twenty to approximately one person in ten | It has been entirely eliminated | It has increased steadily since eighteen hundred and twenty | It has never affected more than one person in a million
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the population of Ukraine has been difficult to determine since two thousand and twenty-two. | Warfare, the departure of refugees and a reduction in births | A refusal by all citizens to be enumerated | The absence of any previous census | The population has in fact been determined with complete precision
